(function(){
COURSE_UNITS.push({"id":"media","num":"14","title":"Build the Giants: Media","color":"orange","lessons":[{"id":"youtube-uploads","title":"How YouTube handles massive video uploads"},{"id":"live-streaming","title":"How live streaming works"},{"id":"netflix-caching","title":"How Netflix caches to hold your attention"}]});
COURSE_LESSONS["youtube-uploads"]={"id":"youtube-uploads","title":"How YouTube handles massive video uploads","summary":"Every minute, creators send hundreds of hours of video. The answer is a factory: ingest the bytes, then let specialised silicon turn one source into a ladder of playable renditions.","minutes":16,"level":"intermediate","goals":[{"text":"Why upload volume is a hardware problem","step":0},{"text":"The rendition ladder and why it exists","step":1},{"text":"Ingest, raw storage and the job queue","step":2},{"text":"Where software encoding ran out of road","step":4},{"text":"How a VCU cluster is organised","step":7},{"text":"Delivery, scheduling and failure modes","step":11}],"steps":[{"short":"The firehose","chapter":"setup","title":"500 hours a minute","body":["A video platform has an unusual unit of work: its input is measured in <b>hours of footage</b>, not requests. YouTube has reported more than <b>500 hours of video arriving every minute</b> — a continuous stream of large, irregular files, each of which must be processed before anybody can watch it.","That number changes the design. There is no single upload request to optimise. There is a flow of jobs whose cost grows with the length and complexity of the footage, and the only way to keep up is to make each hour cheaper to process.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-youtube-handle-massive-video-content-upload.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">How YouTube handles massive video uploads</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"When the workload is measured in media-hours, the unit of capacity is a transcoder, not a web server."},"changes":["+ 500+ video-hours per minute"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"creators","type":"crowd","x0":-15,"x1":-9,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"creators"},{"k":"ing","type":"block","x":-3,"y":0,"label":"ingest","color":"blue"},{"k":"raw","type":"db","x":7,"y":0,"label":"raw object store","sub":"source files"},{"k":"job","type":"queue","x":14,"y":0,"label":"jobs"}],"links":[{"from":"creators","to":"ing","label":"500 h/min"},{"from":"ing","to":"raw","label":"append"},{"from":"raw","to":"job","label":"enqueue"}]}},{"short":"The ladder","chapter":"setup","title":"One upload, many renditions","body":["A phone on a train, a laptop on Wi-Fi and a 4K television cannot all play the same file. So each source is turned into a <b>ladder</b>: several versions at different resolutions and bitrates — roughly 720p for a phone, 1080p for a laptop, 4K for a large screen.","Every rung is the same content encoded at a different cost. The ladder is what lets playback start quickly on a weak connection and look sharp on a strong one. The work is done once, before anyone presses play.","<span class=\"term\">Transcoding</span> is therefore a multiplication step: one input becomes many outputs, and both the compute bill and the storage bill scale with the number of rungs."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Choose the ladder from what viewers actually play — an unused rung is compute and storage spent for nothing."},"changes":["+ 720p / 1080p / 4K renditions"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"src","type":"db","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"source","sub":"one file"},{"k":"enc","type":"block","x":-3,"y":0,"label":"transcode","color":"blue"},{"k":"r1","type":"db","x":7,"y":-8,"label":"720p"},{"k":"r2","type":"db","x":7,"y":0,"label":"1080p"},{"k":"r3","type":"db","x":7,"y":8,"label":"4K"}],"links":[{"from":"src","to":"enc"},{"from":"enc","to":"r1"},{"from":"enc","to":"r2"},{"from":"enc","to":"r3"}]}},{"short":"Ingest","chapter":"technique","title":"Get the bytes in safely","body":["Uploads arrive over ordinary HTTP from unreliable links. The ingest edge accepts a stream in chunks, checkpoints its progress, and lets a dropped connection resume from the last chunk instead of starting over.","The bytes go straight into an <b>object store</b> rather than sitting on an application server. Metadata — owner, title, state — lives in a small database. Keeping bytes and metadata apart means either can be moved, replicated or repaired without disturbing the other."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Resumable, chunked uploads turn a flaky connection into a durable transfer."},"changes":["+ Chunked, resumable ingest","+ Object store for raw bytes","+ Separate metadata store"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"dev","type":"device","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"creator"},{"k":"ing","type":"block","x":-5,"y":0,"label":"ingest edge","color":"blue","sub":"chunk + resume"},{"k":"raw","type":"db","x":4,"y":0,"label":"object store","sub":"raw bytes"},{"k":"meta","type":"db","x":4,"y":-9,"label":"metadata","sub":"owner · state"}],"links":[{"from":"dev","to":"ing","label":"upload"},{"from":"ing","to":"raw","label":"store"},{"from":"ing","to":"meta","label":"record"}]}},{"short":"Job queue","chapter":"technique","title":"Processing starts as a job","body":["Once the last chunk lands, the service gives the video an id and drops a <b>transcode job</b> onto a queue. The uploader is told “processing” rather than being kept waiting: the expensive work is deliberately decoupled from the interactive request.","A queue also gives us retries, priorities and backpressure. A creator uploading during a spike is not blocked behind a long encode, and a worker that crashes simply leaves the job for someone else."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"The queue is the seam between an interactive upload and a batch-heavy pipeline."},"changes":["+ Transcode job queue","~ Upload acknowledgement is immediate"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"raw","type":"db","x":-11,"y":0,"label":"raw object"},{"k":"q","type":"queue","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"job queue"},{"k":"w1","type":"block","x":7,"y":-7,"label":"worker A","color":"blue"},{"k":"w2","type":"block","x":7,"y":7,"label":"worker B","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"raw","to":"q","label":"enqueue"},{"from":"q","to":"w1","label":"claim"},{"from":"q","to":"w2","label":"claim"}]}},{"short":"The wall","chapter":"problem","title":"Why general CPUs ran out of road","body":["Encoding means decoding a frame, deciding how to compress it, then writing a new stream — and doing it again for every rung. On general-purpose CPUs that is slow and expensive, and the bill grows with every uploaded hour.","The pressure grew as video consumption surged: more viewing meant more creators uploading and more demand for fast processing. Adding general servers improved throughput only in proportion to the machines added, while the firehose kept growing.","A task this uniform — the same arithmetic on frame after frame — is exactly the kind of work that rewards custom hardware."],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"If one operation dominates the bill, adding more general-purpose machines moves the cost, not the curve."},"changes":["− Cost per minute on general CPUs","− Throughput headroom"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"jobs","type":"crowd","x0":-15,"x1":-8,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"video-hours"},{"k":"cpu1","type":"block","x":-1,"y":-5,"label":"CPU pool","color":"orange","sub":"software encode"},{"k":"cpu2","type":"block","x":-1,"y":5,"label":"CPU pool","color":"orange","sub":"software encode"},{"k":"out","type":"db","x":11,"y":0,"label":"renditions"}],"links":[{"from":"jobs","to":"cpu1"},{"from":"jobs","to":"cpu2"},{"from":"cpu1","to":"out","label":"slow"},{"from":"cpu2","to":"out","label":"costly"}]}},{"short":"Pipeline","chapter":"technique","title":"Inside the transcode pipeline","body":["A worker reads the source, decodes it, applies compression decisions, encodes each target format, and finally packages the results with a small <b>manifest</b> that describes the available rungs.","Keeping those as separate stages matters operationally: the stage that is slowest can be given more machines without duplicating the whole service, and each stage has its own retry boundary. The same pipeline can also make thumbnails and a preview before the full ladder is ready."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Split the pipeline by cost, not by team — then scale the expensive stage on its own."},"changes":["+ Decode → encode → package stages","+ Manifest of renditions"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"src","type":"db","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"source"},{"k":"dec","type":"block","x":-6,"y":0,"label":"decode","color":"blue"},{"k":"enc","type":"block","x":1,"y":0,"label":"encode ladder","color":"green"},{"k":"pack","type":"block","x":8,"y":0,"label":"package","color":"blue"},{"k":"man","type":"db","x":14,"y":0,"label":"manifest"}],"links":[{"from":"src","to":"dec"},{"from":"dec","to":"enc","label":"frames"},{"from":"enc","to":"pack","label":"rungs"},{"from":"pack","to":"man"}]}},{"short":"VCU","chapter":"insight","title":"A specialised brain for video","body":["The answer published by YouTube in 2021 was the <b>VCU — Video transCoding Unit</b>. Just as a GPU accelerates graphics and a TPU accelerates machine learning, a VCU is silicon built for the one job this pipeline repeats millions of times: encoding and decoding video.","Specialisation pays because the work is narrow and enormous. The chip does far fewer kinds of operation than a CPU, but it does the ones that matter much more efficiently — and that is where the cost of every uploaded hour lives."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"When one operation dominates the bill, build a machine that does only that operation."},"changes":["+ VCU accelerator","− General-purpose CPU encoding"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"job","type":"queue","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"encode jobs"},{"k":"cpu","type":"block","x":-5,"y":-8,"label":"CPU path","color":"orange","sub":"flexible · costly"},{"k":"vcu","type":"block","x":-5,"y":8,"label":"VCU path","color":"green","sub":"narrow · efficient"},{"k":"store","type":"db","x":10,"y":0,"label":"renditions"}],"links":[{"from":"job","to":"cpu"},{"from":"job","to":"vcu"},{"from":"cpu","to":"store","label":"fallback"},{"from":"vcu","to":"store","label":"main path"}]}},{"short":"Machine room","chapter":"technique","title":"Cluster, server, tray, card","body":["The accelerators are organised hierarchically. A <b>cluster</b> holds many VCU-equipped <b>servers</b>; each server carries multiple accelerator <b>trays</b>; each tray holds several <b>VCU cards</b>; and each card has its own encoders and decoders.","The shape is an operational decision as much as a compute one. A failed card is a small unit of repair, a tray can be swapped, and capacity is added a server at a time without changing the software above it."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Design the failure domain as deliberately as the unit of compute."},"changes":["+ Cluster → servers → trays → cards","+ Fine-grained replacement"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"cl","type":"zone","x0":-15,"x1":15,"y0":-11,"y1":11,"label":"one VCU cluster"},{"k":"s1","type":"block","x":-8,"y":-5,"label":"server 1","color":"blue","sub":"trays of cards"},{"k":"s2","type":"block","x":-8,"y":5,"label":"server 2","color":"blue","sub":"trays of cards"},{"k":"t1","type":"block","x":2,"y":-5,"label":"tray","color":"green"},{"k":"t2","type":"block","x":2,"y":5,"label":"tray","color":"green"},{"k":"card","type":"block","x":11,"y":0,"label":"VCU cards","color":"violet","sub":"encoders + decoders"}],"links":[{"from":"s1","to":"t1"},{"from":"s2","to":"t2"},{"from":"t1","to":"card"},{"from":"t2","to":"card"}]}},{"short":"The gain","chapter":"insight","title":"20–33× more work per watt","body":["The reported result of the 2021 design was a <b>20–33× improvement in computing efficiency</b> over the already-optimised software system it replaced.","That is not a nicer number on a slide. The same upload volume costs a fraction of the machines and power, or the same fleet absorbs a much larger firehose. An efficiency jump that large changes which features are affordable, not just how fast they run."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"A 20× improvement in the dominant stage is an architectural move: it redraws the cost model."},"changes":["+ 20–33× computing efficiency","− Cost per uploaded hour"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"Measured gain","lines":["software encode → 1× baseline","VCU cluster   → 20–33×","same 500 h/min, smaller fleet","hardware built for one job"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Store outputs","chapter":"technique","title":"Renditions land in cloud storage","body":["The VCU cluster writes every generated resolution into <b>cloud object storage</b>, alongside the original. Objects are immutable and cheap to replicate, and a rendition is never edited in place — so caches can treat it as permanent.","A metadata service records which renditions exist and the order in which to offer them. Playback decisions read that small index; they never need to inspect the bytes themselves."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Immutable media plus a mutable index is the classic split for very large content stores."},"changes":["+ Immutable rendition objects","+ Rendition index"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"vcu","type":"block","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"VCU cluster","color":"green"},{"k":"obj","type":"db","x":-1,"y":0,"label":"object store","sub":"all rungs"},{"k":"idx","type":"db","x":10,"y":0,"label":"rendition index","sub":"which rungs exist"}],"links":[{"from":"vcu","to":"obj","label":"write"},{"from":"obj","to":"idx","label":"register"}]}},{"short":"Delivery","chapter":"technique","title":"The last mile belongs to the CDN","body":["When a viewer presses play, the player fetches the manifest and asks for the rung that fits its connection. The bytes come from a nearby <b>CDN</b> edge, not from a transcoder: the expensive transformation happened once, and the cheap work of copying bytes happens at the edge.","If bandwidth changes mid-video, the player requests a different rung from the same ladder. The origin mostly sees metadata requests and cache fills, not every frame for every viewer."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Centralise the expensive transformation; distribute the cheap copies."},"changes":["+ CDN edge delivery","+ Adaptive rung selection"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"obj","type":"db","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"origin objects"},{"k":"cdn","type":"zone","x0":-4,"x1":6,"y0":-9,"y1":9,"label":"CDN edges"},{"k":"e1","type":"block","x":-1,"y":-4,"label":"edge · EU","color":"orange"},{"k":"e2","type":"block","x":-1,"y":4,"label":"edge · Asia","color":"orange"},{"k":"view","type":"crowd","x0":11,"x1":16,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"viewers"}],"links":[{"from":"obj","to":"cdn","label":"push"},{"from":"cdn","to":"view","label":"nearby playback"}]}},{"short":"Scheduling","chapter":"technique","title":"Running the fleet","body":["The scheduler decides which job runs where. A freshly uploaded public video may jump ahead of a routine re-encode; a very long 4K source can be split into segments processed in parallel and stitched back together at the end.","Workers are <b>idempotent</b>: replaying a job writes the same output keys rather than creating new ones, so a retry after a crash is safe. Deterministic output names turn failures into ordinary events."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Name outputs from inputs and settings; retries then overwrite instead of duplicating."},"changes":["+ Priority and segment-level parallelism","+ Idempotent output keys"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"q","type":"queue","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"job queue"},{"k":"sched","type":"block","x":-5,"y":0,"label":"scheduler","color":"violet"},{"k":"w1","type":"block","x":3,"y":-7,"label":"VCU worker","color":"green"},{"k":"w2","type":"block","x":3,"y":7,"label":"VCU worker","color":"green"},{"k":"store","type":"db","x":12,"y":0,"label":"object store"}],"links":[{"from":"q","to":"sched"},{"from":"sched","to":"w1","label":"segment A"},{"from":"sched","to":"w2","label":"segment B"},{"from":"w1","to":"store","label":"same keys"},{"from":"w2","to":"store","label":"same keys"}]}},{"short":"Failure modes","chapter":"problem","title":"When uploads go wrong","body":["Sources arrive corrupt, truncated, or in a codec the pipeline cannot read. A validation stage rejects them early, before any expensive encoding is spent. A worker that dies mid-job leaves partial output, so renditions are written under temporary keys and published only when the whole ladder is complete.","A brand-new popular video can also hammer the manifest and the origin before edge caches warm. Request coalescing and short-TTL caching absorb that first rush; the objects themselves stay immutable."],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"An incomplete rung must never be published — publish the whole ladder or none of it."},"changes":["+ Validate before encode","+ Temporary keys, atomic publish","+ Coalesce manifest reads"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"in","type":"device","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"new upload"},{"k":"val","type":"block","x":-6,"y":0,"label":"validate","color":"orange"},{"k":"bad","type":"db","x":-6,"y":-9,"label":"reject","sub":"corrupt · truncated"},{"k":"q","type":"queue","x":1,"y":0,"label":"job queue"},{"k":"tmp","type":"db","x":9,"y":-6,"label":"temporary keys"},{"k":"pub","type":"db","x":9,"y":6,"label":"published rungs"}],"links":[{"from":"in","to":"val"},{"from":"val","to":"bad","label":"invalid"},{"from":"val","to":"q","label":"valid"},{"from":"q","to":"tmp","label":"encode"},{"from":"tmp","to":"pub","label":"publish when complete"}]}},{"short":"Tradeoffs","chapter":"tradeoff","title":"What specialisation costs","body":["Custom silicon is superb at one job and inflexible when codecs change. The software path remains the escape hatch for new formats and awkward edge cases, which means the pipeline has to target an abstraction rather than a particular chip.","There is also storage amplification: every extra rung and format multiplies the bytes retained forever. Measure which renditions are actually played and prune the rest."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Hardware efficiency and storage cost pull in opposite directions — measure both."},"changes":["+ Software fallback for new codecs","− Unused renditions"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The balance","lines":["VCU     → cheap per uploaded hour","software → flexible when codecs change","more rungs → more stored bytes","keep only what viewers play"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Recap","chapter":"recap","title":"Uploads are a factory","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th>S</th><th>t</th><th>a</th><th>g</th><th>e</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ingest</td><td>Chunked, resumable → object store</td></tr><tr><td>Queue</td><td>Decouple upload from processing</td></tr><tr><td>Encode</td><td>VCUs build a rendition ladder</td></tr><tr><td>Store</td><td>Immutable objects plus a small index</td></tr><tr><td>Serve</td><td>CDN edges and adaptive bitrate</td></tr></tbody></table>","The guide's two numbers belong together: 500+ hours a minute is the demand, and 20–33× efficiency is how a very regular pipeline meets it."],"callout":{"kind":"next","text":"Next: <b>How live streaming works</b> — the same ladder, but it has to be ready a few seconds ago."},"changes":null,"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The rule","lines":["upload   → object store","transcode → specialised silicon","outputs  → immutable renditions","playback → CDN + adaptive bitrate"]}],"links":[]}}]};
COURSE_LESSONS["live-streaming"]={"id":"live-streaming","title":"How live streaming works","summary":"A live stream is a factory with a deadline: capture the frames, compress them, chop them into seconds-long pieces and get them to nearby edges before the viewers catch up.","minutes":14,"level":"intermediate","goals":[{"text":"What makes a stream live","step":0},{"text":"Capture, compression and segmentation","step":2},{"text":"Why many bitrates are made at once","step":5},{"text":"CDN fan-out and the player's buffer","step":7},{"text":"Protocols, latency and replay","step":9}],"steps":[{"short":"Live","chapter":"setup","title":"A few seconds behind reality","body":["Ordinary video streaming downloads a file that was finished earlier. <b>Live streaming</b> sends the content over the internet in real time, usually with a latency of only a few seconds — the audience is watching almost as it happens, on YouTube, TikTok Live or Twitch.","That small delay is the whole design constraint. Every stage has a deadline, and a stage that falls behind shows up as a freeze rather than a slower download.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/live-streaming-explained.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Live streaming explained</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Live is not a different video format; it is the same pipeline running against a clock."},"changes":["+ Latency measured in seconds"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"cam","type":"device","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"camera + mic"},{"k":"srv","type":"block","x":-3,"y":0,"label":"streaming server","color":"blue"},{"k":"cdn","type":"block","x":6,"y":0,"label":"CDN edge","color":"orange"},{"k":"v","type":"crowd","x0":12,"x1":16,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"viewers"}],"links":[{"from":"cam","to":"srv","label":"live feed"},{"from":"srv","to":"cdn","label":"segments"},{"from":"cdn","to":"v","label":"seconds later"}]}},{"short":"The route","chapter":"setup","title":"The whole route in one picture","body":["Raw frames leave the camera, are compressed and encoded, then divided into chunks only seconds long. Those chunks go to a streaming server, which pushes them to CDN edge servers; viewers decode and play what they receive from a nearby edge.","If the broadcast should be watchable later, the encoded data is also sent to storage. That replay branch turns a live event into ordinary on-demand video without a second production pipeline."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Two paths leave the encoder: one to the audience now, one to storage for later."},"changes":["+ Capture → encode → segment → edge → player","+ Replay branch to storage"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"cam","type":"device","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"capture"},{"k":"enc","type":"block","x":-8,"y":0,"label":"encode","color":"blue"},{"k":"seg","type":"block","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"segment","color":"green"},{"k":"srv","type":"block","x":4,"y":0,"label":"stream server","color":"blue"},{"k":"cdn","type":"block","x":10,"y":-5,"label":"CDN edge","color":"orange"},{"k":"arch","type":"db","x":10,"y":6,"label":"replay storage"}],"links":[{"from":"cam","to":"enc"},{"from":"enc","to":"seg"},{"from":"seg","to":"srv"},{"from":"srv","to":"cdn","label":"push"},{"from":"srv","to":"arch","label":"record"}]}},{"short":"Capture","chapter":"technique","title":"Getting frames off the camera","body":["A microphone and camera produce raw audio and video. That data is far too large to send as-is, so a local encoder — software on a laptop or a hardware box for a professional setup — encodes it immediately and sends the stream to the ingest server.","The creator's uplink is the first constraint: it is usually slower and less steady than the platform's network, so the encoder must adapt its output bitrate to the link without stopping the show."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"The first encoder is inside the creator's setup, not in the data centre."},"changes":["+ Local encoder","+ Constrained uplink"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"cam","type":"device","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"camera + mic"},{"k":"enc","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"local encoder","color":"green"},{"k":"net","type":"queue","x":5,"y":0,"label":"uplink"},{"k":"in","type":"block","x":13,"y":0,"label":"ingest","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"cam","to":"enc","label":"raw frames"},{"from":"enc","to":"net","label":"compressed"},{"from":"net","to":"in"}]}},{"short":"Compression","chapter":"technique","title":"Make the frames small","body":["Compression removes what the viewer will not notice. One example from the guide is separating the static background from the moving elements, so the background need not be transmitted again and again. Standard codecs such as <b>H.264</b> or <b>H.265</b> then encode what remains.","After this stage the data is dramatically smaller — small enough to push over a home uplink and cheap enough to copy to millions of viewers."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Compression is the lever that makes live video affordable at every later hop."},"changes":["+ Background / foreground separation","+ H.264 or H.265 encoding","− Bytes on every hop"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"raw","type":"db","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"raw frames","sub":"huge"},{"k":"cmp","type":"block","x":-3,"y":0,"label":"compress","color":"green","sub":"remove redundancy"},{"k":"coded","type":"db","x":8,"y":0,"label":"encoded stream","sub":"much smaller"}],"links":[{"from":"raw","to":"cmp"},{"from":"cmp","to":"coded"}]}},{"short":"Segments","chapter":"technique","title":"Chop it into seconds","body":["The encoded stream is divided into short <b>segments</b>, each only a few seconds long. A player can fetch one small piece at a time, start playback quickly, and recover from a network hiccup by requesting the next piece rather than restarting a huge file.","Segments begin at keyframes so each one can be decoded on its own. That independence is what makes switching quality and joining a stream mid-broadcast possible."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Short segments trade a little overhead for fast start, quick recovery and easy joining."},"changes":["+ Seconds-long, self-contained segments","+ Keyframe-aligned starts"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"coded","type":"db","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"encoded stream"},{"k":"cut","type":"block","x":-3,"y":0,"label":"segmenter","color":"green"},{"k":"s1","type":"db","x":6,"y":-8,"label":"seg 1"},{"k":"s2","type":"db","x":6,"y":0,"label":"seg 2"},{"k":"s3","type":"db","x":6,"y":8,"label":"seg 3"}],"links":[{"from":"coded","to":"cut"},{"from":"cut","to":"s1"},{"from":"cut","to":"s2"},{"from":"cut","to":"s3"}]}},{"short":"Bitrates","chapter":"problem","title":"One stream cannot fit every viewer","body":["Viewers have different devices and different networks. A single bitrate would either be too heavy for a phone on mobile data or too blurry for someone on fibre.","The answer is <b>adaptive bitrate streaming</b>: during the compression and segmentation stages the platform produces the same seconds of video at several bitrates, and publishes a manifest listing them. The player picks a rung that its connection can sustain and moves between them as conditions change."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"The ladder must be built live, as the frames arrive — there is no time to prepare it afterwards."},"changes":["+ Multiple bitrates per segment","+ Manifest for the player"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"seg","type":"block","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"segment","color":"green"},{"k":"lad","type":"db","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"bitrate ladder","sub":"3 renditions"},{"k":"man","type":"db","x":8,"y":-8,"label":"manifest"},{"k":"v","type":"device","x":8,"y":6,"label":"player"}],"links":[{"from":"seg","to":"lad"},{"from":"lad","to":"man"},{"from":"man","to":"v","label":"pick rung"}]}},{"short":"Stream server","chapter":"technique","title":"The hub between encoder and audience","body":["The streaming server accepts the incoming segments, makes sure the ladder is complete, packages it for the chosen protocol, and pushes the result towards the CDN. It also handles the ingest side of the protocols a broadcaster uses.","Because it sits at the junction, it is the natural place to detect gaps in the sequence, drop late segments, and keep a small rolling buffer of the most recent seconds."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Keep only a short live window in memory; everything older belongs in the replay store."},"changes":["+ Ingest, package and push","+ Short rolling buffer","+ Gap detection"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"enc","type":"block","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"encoder","color":"green"},{"k":"srv","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"stream server","color":"blue","sub":"package + buffer"},{"k":"cdn","type":"block","x":5,"y":-6,"label":"CDN","color":"orange"},{"k":"arch","type":"db","x":5,"y":6,"label":"replay store"},{"k":"mon","type":"db","x":13,"y":0,"label":"sequence monitor"}],"links":[{"from":"enc","to":"srv"},{"from":"srv","to":"cdn"},{"from":"srv","to":"arch"},{"from":"srv","to":"mon","label":"gaps?"}]}},{"short":"CDN","chapter":"insight","title":"Millions of viewers, one origin","body":["Pushing every frame from one origin to every viewer would collapse under its own fan-out. Instead the segments are delivered to <b>edge servers</b> in the CDN, and viewers fetch them from an edge nearby.","The origin serves each segment to a handful of edges, and the edges serve it to everyone in their region. That dramatically reduces both transmission latency and the load on the broadcaster's infrastructure."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"The CDN turns one expensive upload into many cheap, local downloads."},"changes":["+ Regional edge caches","− Origin fan-out"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"origin","type":"db","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"origin"},{"k":"e1","type":"block","x":-2,"y":-8,"label":"edge · US","color":"orange"},{"k":"e2","type":"block","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"edge · EU","color":"orange"},{"k":"e3","type":"block","x":-2,"y":8,"label":"edge · APAC","color":"orange"},{"k":"v1","type":"crowd","x0":10,"x1":16,"y0":-10,"y1":-3,"label":"viewers"},{"k":"v2","type":"crowd","x0":10,"x1":16,"y0":-2,"y1":2,"label":"viewers"},{"k":"v3","type":"crowd","x0":10,"x1":16,"y0":3,"y1":10,"label":"viewers"}],"links":[{"from":"origin","to":"e1"},{"from":"origin","to":"e2"},{"from":"origin","to":"e3"},{"from":"e1","to":"v1"},{"from":"e2","to":"v2"},{"from":"e3","to":"v3"}]}},{"short":"Player","chapter":"technique","title":"The last few seconds","body":["A player keeps a small buffer of segments — the few seconds that make live streaming feel immediate while leaving room for network jitter. It decodes and decompresses the frames and renders them in the video element.","It watches its own bandwidth and buffer level. If the buffer is draining, it steps down to a lower bitrate; when things recover, it steps back up. A viewer sees a brief change in sharpness instead of a stall."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"The buffer is the shock absorber: bigger means smoother but less live."},"changes":["+ Few-second buffer","+ Bitrate adaptation at the client"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"edge","type":"block","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"CDN edge","color":"orange"},{"k":"buf","type":"queue","x":-3,"y":0,"label":"buffer","sub":"2–6 segments"},{"k":"dec","type":"block","x":5,"y":0,"label":"decode","color":"blue"},{"k":"v","type":"device","x":13,"y":0,"label":"screen"}],"links":[{"from":"edge","to":"buf","label":"download"},{"from":"buf","to":"dec","label":"play out"},{"from":"dec","to":"v","label":"frames"},{"from":"v","to":"buf","label":"bandwidth?","heads":"both"}]}},{"short":"Protocols","chapter":"technique","title":"The vocabulary of live video","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th>P</th><th>r</th><th>o</th><th>t</th><th>o</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RTMP</td><td>Contribution: encoder → streaming server</td></tr><tr><td>HLS</td><td>Apple's format; needs H.264/H.265; accepted by Apple devices</td></tr><tr><td>DASH</td><td>Adaptive over HTTP; not supported on Apple devices</td></tr><tr><td>RTC</td><td>Real-time communication for video calls; lower latency</td></tr></tbody></table>","HLS and DASH both support adaptive bitrate streaming, so the ladder from earlier works with either. Conferencing is a different problem: Skype-style calls use RTC because seconds of delay are unacceptable in a conversation."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Pick the protocol from the audience and the latency budget, not from habit."},"changes":["+ RTMP for ingest","+ HLS / DASH for delivery","+ RTC for conversations"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"Two delivery families","lines":["HLS  → H.264/H.265, Apple-friendly","DASH → adaptive, not on Apple","both → multiple bitrates","RTC  → calls, much lower latency"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Latency","chapter":"problem","title":"Where the seconds go","body":["Live latency is the sum of the pipeline: capture, encode, segment, distribute to edges, then the player's buffer. Each stage adds a slice, and cutting one slice often makes another harder.","A longer buffer smooths out network jitter but pushes the stream further behind reality. Shorter segments reduce delay but increase request overhead. The design target is a few seconds, not zero — the budget is spent deliberately."],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"You cannot remove the buffer without giving up smoothness; you can only choose where the delay sits."},"changes":["+ Explicit latency budget across stages","− Buffer size → + smoothness risk"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"A few seconds, allocated","lines":["capture + encode   → some ms","segments           → a segment or two","edge distribution  → network time","player buffer      → jitter cushion","total              → a few seconds"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Replay","chapter":"technique","title":"Keep it for later","body":["If a broadcast should be available afterwards, the encoded data is sent to a storage server as well as to the audience. Viewers can then request a replay from that store.","Because the stored copy is already segmented and already has a bitrate ladder, it behaves like ordinary on-demand video. The live pipeline has done most of the work of producing the replay as a side effect."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Recording is a branch off the encoder, not a second system."},"changes":["+ Segments written to storage","+ Replay uses the same ladder"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"enc","type":"block","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"encoder","color":"green"},{"k":"live","type":"block","x":-3,"y":-7,"label":"live → CDN","color":"orange"},{"k":"store","type":"db","x":-3,"y":7,"label":"segment storage"},{"k":"now","type":"device","x":8,"y":-7,"label":"watching now"},{"k":"later","type":"device","x":8,"y":7,"label":"replay viewer"}],"links":[{"from":"enc","to":"live"},{"from":"enc","to":"store","label":"record"},{"from":"live","to":"now"},{"from":"store","to":"later","label":"on demand"}]}},{"short":"Stutter","chapter":"problem","title":"When the uplink or the network stutters","body":["Frames can be dropped at capture, an uplink can stall, and an edge can miss a segment. The sequence numbering on segments makes gaps visible; the server can request a resend while it is still in the rolling buffer, or skip forward with a keyframe.","On the viewer side a draining buffer triggers a lower-bitrate request; an empty buffer is a stall. Bounded queues and backpressure stop one slow region from growing without limit, and the replay store is the durable copy if the live window has already moved on."],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"Live delivery is best-effort; the recorded segments are the durable truth."},"changes":["+ Sequence gaps detected","+ Bounded buffers and backpressure","+ Keyframe recovery"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"enc","type":"block","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"encoder","color":"green"},{"k":"buf","type":"queue","x":-5,"y":0,"label":"rolling window","color":"red"},{"k":"edge","type":"block","x":3,"y":0,"label":"CDN edge","color":"orange"},{"k":"v","type":"device","x":12,"y":0,"label":"player"},{"k":"store","type":"db","x":-5,"y":-9,"label":"replay store","sub":"durable"}],"links":[{"from":"enc","to":"buf","label":"gap?"},{"from":"buf","to":"edge","label":"recent only"},{"from":"edge","to":"v","label":"buffer drains"},{"from":"buf","to":"store","label":"keep"}]}},{"short":"Tradeoffs","chapter":"tradeoff","title":"Choosing the compromise","body":["HLS is required for Apple devices; DASH is not supported there but is widely used elsewhere. Both deliver adaptive bitrate, so the choice is about reach and tooling as much as technology.","Across the whole pipeline, latency, picture quality and cost pull against each other: more rungs and shorter segments mean more encode work and more requests. Broadcasts tolerate seconds because that is what makes millions of viewers affordable; conversations do not, which is why they use RTC."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"The right latency target comes from the experience, then the pipeline is engineered to hit it."},"changes":["+ HLS for Apple reach","+ DASH elsewhere","− Latency where it is not needed"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"Three dials","lines":["lower latency  → less buffering","higher quality → more encode + bytes","more viewers  → more edges, more cache","pick the target, then spend the budget"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Recap","chapter":"recap","title":"Follow the segments","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th>S</th><th>t</th><th>a</th><th>g</th><th>e</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capture</td><td>Camera and mic feed a local encoder</td></tr><tr><td>Compress</td><td>H.264/H.265 makes frames small</td></tr><tr><td>Segment</td><td>Seconds-long, keyframe-aligned chunks</td></tr><tr><td>Adapt</td><td>Several bitrates plus a manifest</td></tr><tr><td>Distribute</td><td>CDN edges to nearby viewers</td></tr><tr><td>Replay</td><td>Encoded segments stored for later</td></tr></tbody></table>","Every arrow is a place where seconds can be lost — and a place where the pipeline must keep up with the clock."],"callout":{"kind":"next","text":"Next: <b>How Netflix caches to hold your attention</b> — the same concern with a different metric: the next ninety seconds."},"changes":null,"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The route","lines":["capture → compress → segment","segment → ladder → CDN edge","player  → buffer → decode","encoder → storage → replay"]}],"links":[]}}]};
COURSE_LESSONS["netflix-caching"]={"id":"netflix-caching","title":"How Netflix caches to hold your attention","summary":"Four very different jobs, one distributed cache: Netflix uses EVCache as a read-through layer, a session store, a home-page database and a publishing bus — all to keep the next click fast.","minutes":14,"level":"intermediate","goals":[{"text":"Why latency is a product metric","step":0},{"text":"Where EVCache sits in the architecture","step":1},{"text":"Lookaside reads and their traps","step":2},{"text":"Sessions, precomputed homes and UI strings","step":4},{"text":"What the cache does not hold","step":9},{"text":"Failure modes and trade-offs","step":11}],"steps":[{"short":"90 seconds","chapter":"setup","title":"The shortest budget in the product","body":["Netflix's stated goal is to keep you streaming for as long as possible, while a viewer's attention span is often cited as roughly <b>90 seconds</b>. If the next screen hesitates, the viewer is gone.","That turns latency into a product metric, not an engineering nicety. The response to it is <b>EVCache</b>, a distributed in-memory key-value store used in several quite different ways.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/4-ways-netflix-uses-caching-to-hold-user-attention.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">4 ways Netflix uses caching</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/netflixs-overall-architecture.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Netflix's overall architecture</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"When attention is the scarce resource, a cache miss is a business event."},"changes":["+ 90-second attention budget","+ EVCache as the latency lever"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"v","type":"device","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"viewer"},{"k":"app","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"app","color":"blue"},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":5,"y":0,"label":"EVCache","sub":"milliseconds"},{"k":"next","type":"device","x":13,"y":0,"label":"next screen"}],"links":[{"from":"v","to":"app","label":"tap"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","label":"fast read"},{"from":"cache","to":"next","label":"instant"}]}},{"short":"The architecture","chapter":"setup","title":"Where the cache sits","body":["The wider architecture explains why so many services depend on one cache. Native apps are built in Swift and Kotlin, the web app in React, and the two talk to the backend through <b>GraphQL</b>. Behind that sit services built with Zuul, Eureka and Spring Boot, with data in EVCache, Cassandra and CockroachDB.","Kafka and Flink carry events and streams, S3 and Open Connect hold the video itself, and Flink/Spark feed analytics. EVCache is the shared fast layer that the online services read on the critical path.","<span class=\"src\">Also grounded in ByteByteGo's <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/netflixs-overall-architecture.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Netflix's overall architecture</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"A cache in the centre of many read paths is a shared dependency: treat its API and its failure modes as product surface."},"changes":["+ GraphQL front door","+ Services + EVCache/Cassandra","+ Kafka/Flink and S3/Open Connect"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"cli","type":"device","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"web · mobile"},{"k":"gql","type":"block","x":-7,"y":0,"label":"GraphQL","color":"blue"},{"k":"svc","type":"block","x":0,"y":0,"label":"services","color":"blue","sub":"Zuul · Eureka"},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":8,"y":-7,"label":"EVCache"},{"k":"cas","type":"db","x":8,"y":7,"label":"Cassandra / DBs"},{"k":"video","type":"db","x":15,"y":0,"label":"S3 + Open Connect"}],"links":[{"from":"cli","to":"gql"},{"from":"gql","to":"svc"},{"from":"svc","to":"cache","label":"fast path"},{"from":"svc","to":"cas","label":"slower path"},{"from":"svc","to":"video","label":"bytes"}]}},{"short":"Lookaside","chapter":"technique","title":"Use one: the lookaside cache","body":["The familiar pattern is a <b>lookaside cache</b>. The application asks the EVCache client for a key. On a hit it uses the value immediately; on a miss it calls the backend service, which in turn reads Cassandra, returns the data and fills the cache for next time.","The cache is not the source of truth — it is a shortcut in front of one. That makes failures recoverable: lose the cache and the service can still answer, just more slowly."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Lookaside keeps correctness in the database and speed in the cache."},"changes":["+ Cache-aside read path","+ Miss → service → Cassandra → fill"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"app","type":"device","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"application"},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"EVCache"},{"k":"svc","type":"block","x":6,"y":-6,"label":"backend service","color":"blue"},{"k":"db","type":"db","x":14,"y":-6,"label":"Cassandra"}],"links":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","label":"1 get"},{"from":"cache","to":"svc","label":"2 miss"},{"from":"svc","to":"db","label":"3 query"},{"from":"svc","to":"cache","label":"4 fill"}]}},{"short":"Lookaside traps","chapter":"problem","title":"Misses can multiply","body":["A lookaside cache has sharp edges. When a very popular key expires, many requests miss at once and all stampede the backend — the classic <b>cache stampede</b>. Stale values are another hazard when a service updates the database but forgets to refresh or invalidate the entry.","The usual defences are single-flight refills so one request does the work, short TTLs with jitter so keys do not expire together, and explicit invalidation on writes."],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"A cache is a performance tool; if correctness depends on it being fresh, you have built a different system."},"changes":["+ Single-flight refill","+ TTL jitter","+ Invalidate on write"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"crowd","type":"crowd","x":-15,"y":0,"label":"many requests","x0":-15,"x1":-9,"y0":-8,"y1":8},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":-3,"y":0,"label":"EVCache","sub":"key expired"},{"k":"svc","type":"block","x":6,"y":0,"label":"backend","color":"red","sub":"stampede"},{"k":"db","type":"db","x":14,"y":0,"label":"Cassandra"}],"links":[{"from":"crowd","to":"cache","label":"all miss"},{"from":"cache","to":"svc","label":"N refills"},{"from":"svc","to":"db"}]}},{"short":"Sessions","chapter":"technique","title":"Use two: transient playback state","body":["Netflix also uses EVCache as a <b>transient data store</b>. Playback session information — where a viewer is in a title, what was played, what comes next — is written by one service and read or updated by others as the session moves through the system.","One application service may start the session, another may update it, and a third closes it at the end. Keeping that state in a shared fast store lets those services stay independent."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"A short-lived session is a good cache citizen: it has a natural expiry and no long-term truth to protect."},"changes":["+ Shared playback session","+ Multiple services, one key"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"s1","type":"block","x":-13,"y":-7,"label":"start service","color":"blue"},{"k":"s2","type":"block","x":-13,"y":7,"label":"update service","color":"blue"},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":0,"y":0,"label":"EVCache","sub":"session key · TTL"},{"k":"s3","type":"block","x":12,"y":0,"label":"close service","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"s1","to":"cache","label":"create"},{"from":"s2","to":"cache","label":"update"},{"from":"cache","to":"s3","label":"read / close"}]}},{"short":"Session keys","chapter":"insight","title":"Design for expiry","body":["A session key needs a stable identity, a short TTL and a clear owner of each write. TTL means a crashed service cannot leave state behind forever; a stable key means any service can find the session again.","The important boundary: this is convenient playback state, not the billing or entitlement record. Anything that must survive a cache flush belongs in a durable store, with the cache only accelerating reads of it."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Decide per field: is it transient convenience, or a fact you cannot lose?"},"changes":["+ Stable session id + short TTL","− Durable truth in the cache"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"Session rules","lines":["key      → viewer + device + play id","value    → position · state · next","ttl      → short, refreshed on activity","truth    → durable store, cache is a shortcut"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Precomputed home","chapter":"technique","title":"Use three: the cache as the database","body":["The most surprising use is as a <b>primary store</b>. Large pre-computation jobs run every night and build a brand-new home page for every profile of every user, using watch history and recommendations. All of that output is written into the EVCache cluster.","Online services then read the finished home page from EVCache when a viewer opens the app. On the read path, the cache is the only store that matters — the expensive personalisation already happened offline."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Move the work to a quiet time, then make the read a lookup."},"changes":["+ Nightly per-profile precompute","+ EVCache as read primary","− Online personalisation cost"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"hist","type":"db","x":-14,"y":-7,"label":"watch history"},{"k":"rec","type":"block","x":-5,"y":-7,"label":"recommenders","color":"violet"},{"k":"batch","type":"block","x":3,"y":-7,"label":"nightly precompute","color":"green"},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":11,"y":0,"label":"EVCache","sub":"home per profile"},{"k":"v","type":"device","x":11,"y":8,"label":"viewer"}],"links":[{"from":"hist","to":"rec"},{"from":"rec","to":"batch"},{"from":"batch","to":"cache","label":"write"},{"from":"v","to":"cache","label":"read"}]}},{"short":"Cache as primary","chapter":"insight","title":"What that changes","body":["When the cache is the primary store, a miss is no longer a slow read — it is a missing home page. That demands a rebuild path, versioned keys so a new build can be published atomically, and a last-known-good copy if a cluster is unavailable.","In exchange, the interactive path becomes wonderfully boring: one fast key lookup, no fan-out across services, no variability in what a viewer sees while it is assembled."],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"Treating a cache as a database means owning the rebuild, the rollback and the cold-start story."},"changes":["+ Versioned home keys","+ Rebuild + last-known-good","+ Predictable read latency"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"build","type":"block","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"home builder","color":"green"},{"k":"v1","type":"db","x":-3,"y":-7,"label":"home v1"},{"k":"v2","type":"db","x":-3,"y":7,"label":"home v2"},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":7,"y":0,"label":"EVCache","sub":"publish pointer"},{"k":"v","type":"device","x":15,"y":0,"label":"viewer"}],"links":[{"from":"build","to":"v1"},{"from":"build","to":"v2"},{"from":"build","to":"cache","label":"swap pointer"},{"from":"cache","to":"v"}]}},{"short":"Strings","chapter":"technique","title":"Use four: high-volume, always-on data","body":["The fourth pattern targets data that is read constantly and must be highly available: UI strings and translations shown on the home page. A separate process computes and publishes them <b>asynchronously</b> into EVCache.","The application then reads them from the cache with low latency even if the source system is slow. Publishing is off the request path, so a refresh cannot stall a viewer's screen."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Asynchronous publication is ideal when the data is small, read everywhere and can be a moment stale."},"changes":["+ Async string publication","+ Low-latency, high-availability reads"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"src","type":"db","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"string source"},{"k":"pub","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"publisher","color":"green","sub":"async"},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":5,"y":0,"label":"EVCache","sub":"UI strings"},{"k":"apps","type":"crowd","x0":12,"x1":16,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"services"}],"links":[{"from":"src","to":"pub"},{"from":"pub","to":"cache","label":"publish"},{"from":"cache","to":"apps","label":"read"}]}},{"short":"Not the bytes","chapter":"insight","title":"What EVCache does not hold","body":["It is easy to assume the whole catalog sits in EVCache. It does not: the video files live in <b>S3 and Open Connect</b>, the CDN Netflix operates to serve content near viewers.","The cache holds the small things that decide what to show next — home pages, strings, sessions, metadata lookups. Large immutable video bytes are delivered from edges. Separating the two means the cache stays small and fast while the bytes scale independently."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Cache decisions, not payloads: the small data on the critical path is what latency is made of."},"changes":["+ Metadata and UI state in EVCache","+ Video bytes from S3 / Open Connect"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"app","type":"block","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"service","color":"blue"},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":-2,"y":-7,"label":"EVCache","sub":"decisions"},{"k":"db","type":"db","x":-2,"y":7,"label":"databases","sub":"facts"},{"k":"cdn","type":"db","x":10,"y":0,"label":"Open Connect","sub":"video bytes"},{"k":"v","type":"device","x":16,"y":0,"label":"viewer"}],"links":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","label":"fast"},{"from":"app","to":"db","label":"durable"},{"from":"cdn","to":"v","label":"stream"},{"from":"app","to":"v","label":"what to play"}]}},{"short":"Data plane","chapter":"technique","title":"How the cache gets filled","body":["Nothing in the cache appears by magic. Kafka and Flink carry event streams, Spark and Flink process them, and the results feed the recommenders and the nightly jobs that write home pages and UI data into EVCache.","The surrounding tooling — deployment pipelines, chaos testing and monitoring — exists to keep that machinery honest. A cache this central needs to be treated as a product with owners, not a bag of keys."],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Treat the cache fill pipeline as part of the system's correctness, not as background plumbing."},"changes":["+ Kafka / Flink events","+ Spark/Flink processing → cache fill","+ Ownership and observability"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"ev","type":"queue","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"events · Kafka"},{"k":"flink","type":"block","x":-6,"y":0,"label":"Flink / Spark","color":"violet"},{"k":"jobs","type":"block","x":2,"y":0,"label":"precompute jobs","color":"green"},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":10,"y":0,"label":"EVCache"}],"links":[{"from":"ev","to":"flink"},{"from":"flink","to":"jobs"},{"from":"jobs","to":"cache","label":"publish"}]}},{"short":"Failures","chapter":"problem","title":"When the fast layer fails","body":["An EVCache outage removes the shortcut, not necessarily the product — a lookaside read falls through to services and databases, which must have the capacity to absorb the extra load. A primary-store home page has no such fallback, so it needs versioned rebuilds and a last-known-good copy.","Other sharp edges: a cold new profile with no precomputed page, a stampede after a deploy invalidates many keys, and stale strings after a late publication. Each has a different fix, which is why the four patterns need separate runbooks."],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"A shared cache concentrates risk: know for each key whether losing it means slower or missing."},"changes":["+ Fall-through capacity planning","+ Versioned rebuild + last-known-good","+ Warm new profiles"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"v","type":"device","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"viewer"},{"k":"cache","type":"db","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"EVCache","sub":"DOWN","color":"red"},{"k":"svc","type":"block","x":5,"y":-7,"label":"service fallback","color":"blue"},{"k":"rebuild","type":"block","x":5,"y":7,"label":"rebuild job","color":"green"},{"k":"db","type":"db","x":14,"y":0,"label":"durable store"}],"links":[{"from":"v","to":"cache","label":"miss"},{"from":"cache","to":"svc","label":"fall through"},{"from":"svc","to":"db"},{"from":"rebuild","to":"cache","label":"restore"}]}},{"short":"Tradeoffs","chapter":"tradeoff","title":"One tool, four contracts","body":["The elegance of the design is that one distributed store serves four roles — but each role has a different contract. A lookaside entry may vanish harmlessly; a precomputed home cannot. A session can expire; a UI string must still be there.","That sets the trade-offs: speed against correctness, precompute cost against read simplicity, and flexibility against the discipline of owning rebuild paths. Naming the role of each key is the first step to designing it well."],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Before adding a key, ask which of the four contracts it lives under."},"changes":["+ Four clear roles","− One-size-fits-all TTLs","+ Explicit ownership"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"Four contracts","lines":["lookaside  → may vanish, fall through","session    → expires on purpose","primary    → must be rebuilt and versioned","published  → async, momentarily stale"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Recap","chapter":"recap","title":"Latency is a feature","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th>S</th><th>t</th><th>a</th><th>g</th><th>e</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lookaside</td><td>Cache first, backend on miss, then fill</td></tr><tr><td>Transient</td><td>Short-lived playback sessions shared by services</td></tr><tr><td>Primary</td><td>Nightly precomputed home pages read online</td></tr><tr><td>High volume</td><td>Async-published UI strings and translations</td></tr><tr><td>Not here</td><td>Video bytes come from S3 and Open Connect</td></tr></tbody></table>","The common thread is the 90-second budget: move expensive work off the interactive path, then make what remains a fast, predictable lookup."],"callout":{"kind":"next","text":"That's Build the Giants: Media. Next: <b>Build the Giants: Social &amp; Mail</b> — feeds, inboxes and what happens when everyone refreshes at once."},"changes":null,"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The rule","lines":["small data  → EVCache on the critical path","video bytes → S3 + Open Connect edges","expensive work → nightly or async","role per key → correctness contract"]}],"links":[]}}]};
})();
